{"name":"Swampland","hex":"#226633","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"swampland","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Low-lying land that is regularly flooded; especially such land that is drier than a bog or a marsh.","The set of effective low-energy physical theories that are not compatible with quantum gravity."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/swampland"}],"entries":[{"title":"Swampland (physics)","blocks":[{"text":"In physics, the term swampland refers to effective low-energy physical theories which are not compatible with quantum gravity. This is in contrast with the so-called \"string theory landscape\" that are known to be compatible with string theory, which is hypothesized to be a consistent quantum theory of gravity. In other words, the Swampland is the set of consistent-looking theories with no consistent ultraviolet completion with the addition of gravity."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swampland_(physics)","wikidata":"Q7653378"},{"title":"Swamp","blocks":[{"text":"A swamp is a forested wetland. Swamps are considered to be transition zones because both land and water play a role in creating this environment. Swamps vary in size and are located all around the world. The water of a swamp may be fresh water, brackish water, or seawater. Freshwater swamps form along large rivers or lakes where they are critically dependent upon rainwater and seasonal flooding to maintain natural water level fluctuations. Saltwater swamps are found along tropical and subtropical coastlines. Some swamps have hammocks, or dry-land protrusions, covered by aquatic vegetation, or vegetation that tolerates periodic inundation or soil saturation. The two main types of swamp are \"true\" or swamp forests and \"transitional\" or shrub swamps. In the boreal regions of Canada, the word swamp is colloquially used for what is more formally termed a bog, fen, or muskeg. Some of the world's largest swamps are found along major rivers such as the Amazon, the Mississippi, and the Congo."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Florida_freshwater_swamp_usgov_image.jpg/330px-Florida_freshwater_swamp_usgov_image.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Swamp","wikidata":"Q166735"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Coastal_marsh_profile.png/500px-Coastal_marsh_profile.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Coastal_marsh_profile.png/500px-Coastal_marsh_profile.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Infographic demonstrating transition between swamp and marsh, among the nomenclature of other portions of a costal wetland environment","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACoastal_marsh_profile.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg/1280px-Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4b/Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg/500px-Linnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The Linnaistensuo Mire, a nature reserve swamp in Lahti, Finland.[9]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALinnaistensuo_swamp%2C_Lahti%2C_Finland.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Marsh_Arabs_in_a_mashoof.jpg/500px-Marsh_Arabs_in_a_mashoof.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pa